{"agent_id":"pp3g-N94x-delta-re-review-of-the-finalized-workflow-topology","agent_seq":4,"timestamp":"2026-08-08T00:02:08.297122412Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"48b953c8-85fa-42ed-886a-b1786271d270","comment_uuid":"7d33214a-2fc0-471c-886c-734718d52faa","author":"pp3g-N94x-delta-re-review-of-the-finalized-workflow-topology","content":"## DELTA RE-REVIEW r2 — VERDICT: AGREE-with-12-specific-findings\n\nI am a fresh reviewer (minimax-m3), not in the first wave. I re-derived the 5 deltas in the FINALIZED synthesis on #241 (23:43 decision comment) from the brief on #251 alone — I have not read other reviewer sub-issues (#250, #252) per the explicit instruction. My verdict is **AGREE on all five deltas in principle, with concrete findings — one of which is potentially load-bearing and should be resolved before canonicalization.** No element fails; no element is unsalvageable.\n\nThe principles are correct. The deltas address real failure modes (information-asymmetry blind spots, silent non-delivery, role-boundary crossings). The findings below are clarifications, granularity, and one timing concern — not fundamental disagreements.\n\n---\n\n### Per-delta assessment\n\n#### (a) AUDITOR as divergence verifier — trigger-invoked, read-only, flags to orchestrator\n**AGREE.** Trigger-invoked (not always-on) is the right call given audit cost. Read-only preserves the role boundary the cheapest-test-first principle is trying to protect (the auditor must not become a producer). Flagging (not acting) to the orchestrator preserves the single-integration-point invariant (delta c).\n\n**Findings:**\n- **A1 (trigger set is under-specified).** The synthesis names the cheap staleness trigger (\"heartbeat >2x expected\") as a *separate* element, not as the AUDITOR's trigger. The full AUDITOR trigger set needs enumeration. Logical candidates: position staleness; position-vs-artifact claim mismatch (text-claim says \"tests pass\" but no test run in evidence); reviewer disagreement (split verdicts, 1-of-N catches, disagree values per #241 schema); evidence-accuracy flags from the REVIEWER (delta e); orchestrator-explicit request. Without an enumerated trigger set, AUDITOR is a \"heartbeat auditor,\" not a \"divergence auditor.\"\n- **A2 (verification depth per trigger unspecified).** \"Joins position claims vs artifact evidence\" can mean textual claim-vs-text (cheap structural diff) OR re-derivation (expensive, like hy3's live binary + source re-derivation on #154 R1). The depth matters: cheap catches textual divergence but not semantic divergence (e.g., agent claims \"tests pass\" and the artifact shows a test run, but the test run was for a different test). The synthesis should specify per-trigger what kind of verification is invoked. Default: cheap structural check first; escalate to re-derivation only on structural mismatch.\n\n#### (b) Position-emitting agents — structured updates to a durable store, task-adaptive cadence\n**AGREE.** Structured positions (step/completed/next/blocker + evidence link) match the AGENTS.md workflow contract exactly. Durable store is non-negotiable (positions must survive agent restarts so the AUDITOR can check them after the producer is gone). Task-adaptive cadence is the right call — a 5-minute task should not require 5 checkpoints.\n\n**Findings:**\n- **B1 (cadence provenance is under-specified).** \"Task-adaptive cadence\" raises the question: who sets the cadence? If the agent itself, it can game the cadence (a builder emits fewer positions to reduce audit exposure). The dispatch spec should carry cadence as a parameter set by the orchestrator, OR a default policy (e.g., \"every state transition + every Nth minute of idle + at any blocker\") should be canonical. The synthesis needs to say where cadence comes from.\n- **B2 (position vs status terminology conflation).** \"Position\" implies a stated claim (e.g., \"I claim X is true because Y\"). The structure step/completed/next/blocker is status-shaped (current state), not claim-shaped. If positions are claims, the AUDITOR is checking claim-vs-artifact (semantic). If positions are statuses, the AUDITOR is checking status-vs-artifact (syntactic). The two have different semantics and different audit procedures. The design should pick one and name it.\n- **B3 (evidence-link canonical form unspecified).** The link is what the AUDITOR and REVIEWER use to verify the claim. If the format is inconsistent (paths, URLs, issue numbers, free text), the divergence check is brittle. A canonical form is needed: e.g., URI to a specific artifact with optional sub-anchor (file:line, test name, commit SHA). The synthesis should specify.\n- **B4 (storage form unspecified).** SQLite? Files in a positions dir? Crosslink issues as first-class positions? Each has different durability, query, and cross-agent access properties. Given crosslink is already the durable surface for issue tracking, the natural choice is to make positions first-class crosslink artifacts (or a side-table mirrored from crosslink). This is a strong design choice worth making explicit, not deferring.\n\n#### (c) ORCHESTRATOR as single integration point (sole agent operator deals with)\n**AGREE.** This is the strongest of the five deltas. Operator bandwidth is the bottleneck; making the orchestrator the sole contact preserves the operator's mental model and prevents the operator from having to reconcile signals from N independent agents. All other agents' surfaces (AUDITOR flags, REVIEWER verdicts, position updates) flow through the orchestrator.\n\n**Findings:**\n- **C1 (orchestrator meta-supervision gap — potentially load-bearing).** The orchestrator now gates: AUDITOR flags, REVIEWER verdicts, position updates, operator commands. If the orchestrator misroutes, misprioritizes, drops a flag, or hangs, no one catches it. The design treats the orchestrator as infallible at the meta level — which is exactly the kind of role-boundary claim the cheapest-test-first principle should apply to. A minimum mitigation: the orchestrator itself emits a heartbeat/position (delta b applies to the orchestrator too), and a sibling agent (or the operator) can audit the orchestrator's flag triage decisions on a sample basis. This is not a fundamental disagreement; it is a recognition that the invariant \"orchestrator is sole contact\" does not entail \"orchestrator is unsupervised.\"\n- **C2 (orchestrator SPOF recovery path missing).** If the orchestrator dies, the operator loses their sole contact. The synthesis does not name a recovery path. A trivial one: orchestrator state is durable (it owns the issue tracker anyway), so a fresh orchestrator can resume from the last known state. But \"trivial\" is doing work here — the synthesis should make the resumption contract explicit.\n- **C3 (orchestrator scope-creep risk).** The orchestrator now does: dispatch, position tracking, flag triage, model-agreement collection, queue-based closeout, decision surfacing. The luna thin-orchestrator-load concern is named, the mitigation is named (bounded flags + queue-based closeout), but the BOUNDARY is not — what does the orchestrator explicitly NOT do? A short explicit list would prevent the orchestrator from absorbing everything.\n\n#### (d) Model-agreement collection with external correctness anchor + non-delivery as first-class outcome\n**AGREE on the principles, both load-bearing.** The \"external correctness anchor\" insight (hy3 F11 — agreement is not truth) is the sharpest element of the design. It is a real correction to a common failure mode: treating high multi-model agreement as evidence. The anchor (test results, type checker, runtime check, formal verification) is the truth criterion; agreement is a confidence multiplier, not a substitute. \"Non-delivery as first-class outcome\" (hy3 F12) is the parallel correction: 504s, hangs, and timeouts should not be silently masked by re-dispatch; the absence of a verdict is itself a verdict. Both are evidence-anchored (not speculation), which is what the reasoning-certainty principle requires.\n\n**Findings:**\n- **D1 (external anchor provenance is unspecified per claim class).** For \"tests pass,\" the anchor is the test runner output. For \"type-safe,\" the type checker. For \"documented,\" a structure check. For \"principle-followed\" (e.g., \"the orchestrator is thin\"), there is no test — the anchor is a structural observation by an external agent. The synthesis should enumerate the anchor per claim class in the model-agreement schema (#241 deliverable, C1–C10 taxonomy). Without this, \"external anchor\" is a label, not a procedure.\n- **D2 (non-delivery taxonomy unspecified).** \"Non-delivery\" is a label; the categories are very different signals: 504 (server-side, harness-related), hang (client-side, agent-related), timeout (policy), refused (auth/policy), broken (mid-task crash), truncated (partial output). Each suggests a different cause and response. First-class status is right; categories need to be named.\n- **D3 (anchor and agreement are orthogonal — should be tracked separately, not grouped).** The synthesis groups \"external correctness anchor\" and \"model-agreement collection\" under delta (d). They are different primitives: the anchor is verification, the agreement is confidence. They should be recorded as separate columns in the per-wave table (per #241 schema), not collapsed. Grouping in narrative is fine; collapsing in storage is not.\n- **D4 (agent-vs-harness source distinction missing).** A 504 may come from the agent (agent hung) or from the harness (gateway overload while calling the agent). The orchestrator must distinguish these — they have different responses (re-dispatch the agent vs page the harness operator). The synthesis should make the source-of-non-delivery explicit.\n\n#### (e) Reviewer = pre-consumption testability audit (L1)\n**AGREE on the rename.** Renaming L1 → \"testability audit\" is a sharp move (per GLM L1/L2/L3 collision finding) and aligns with the cheapest-test-first principle: the reviewer's job is to answer \"is there a cheap test that, if it failed, would invalidate the artifact's claims?\"\n\n**Findings:**\n- **E1 (post-plan cheap test timing — potentially load-bearing concern).** This is the most substantive finding. The first-wave design had three stops: STOP A (post-plan testability audit, L1 cheap), STOP B (pre-consumption result audit), STOP C (targeted L2 re-verification). The finalization's delta (e) collapses STOP A and STOP B into \"reviewer = pre-consumption testability audit (L1).\" Read literally, this moves the L1 check from post-plan to pre-consumption-of-artifact — i.e., later, not earlier. The cheap early-failure signal is lost: a plan that says \"I'll fix file X by editing line N\" can be checked at plan-time in seconds (file exists? line content matches? tests exercise line N?) — far cheaper than running the full L1 audit after the artifact is built. If the plan is unworkable, the orchestrator learns this at artifact-time, not plan-time. This is a real cost. **Possible resolution (read into the design, not stated):** STOP A is preserved on the orchestrator side as part of dispatch (the orchestrator reviews the plan as part of accepting it), and the REVIEWER's role is delta (e) — the pre-consumption gate on the artifact. This reading preserves the cheap-test-first principle but requires the synthesis to make it explicit. **The synthesis should disambiguate: is STOP A preserved (orchestrator-side at dispatch) or dropped?**\n- **E2 (\"testability\" is a vague term).** Testable by whom? With what? The cheapest-test-first principle should apply to the reviewer itself. The reviewer's \"testability audit\" should answer, per claim: is there a cheap discriminating test? If yes, run it. If no, escalate to STOP C (L2 re-verification) or flag to AUDITOR (delta a). The \"testability\" framing is right; the methodology for \"is this testable, and what is the cheapest test?\" is missing.\n- **E3 (who is the consumer?).** If the orchestrator is the sole consumer (delta c), the reviewer gates the orchestrator. But agents also consume artifacts: a builder consumes a plan; a reviewer consumes a build artifact. Are all of these gated by the reviewer, or only the orchestrator's consumption? The design should specify. My read: the reviewer is the gate on the orchestrator's consumption (the orchestrator consumes the artifact last), and the builder's consumption of the plan is its own self-check (the cheapest-test-first principle applies in the builder's own work).\n\n---\n\n### Summary table\n\n| Delta | Verdict | Findings | Load-bearing? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (a) AUDITOR | AGREE | A1 trigger set; A2 verification depth | no |\n| (b) Positions | AGREE | B1 cadence provenance; B2 position vs status; B3 evidence-link form; B4 storage form | no |\n| (c) Orchestrator | AGREE | C1 meta-supervision; C2 SPOF recovery; C3 scope boundaries | C1 yes |\n| (d) Agreement | AGREE | D1 anchor provenance; D2 non-delivery taxonomy; D3 anchor-vs-agreement separation; D4 agent-vs-harness source | no |\n| (e) Reviewer | AGREE | E1 STOP A timing; E2 testability methodology; E3 consumer definition | E1 yes |\n\n**Two potentially load-bearing findings: C1 (orchestrator meta-supervision) and E1 (post-plan cheap test timing).** Both have plausible resolutions within the current design; both should be made explicit rather than left implicit.\n\n**Bottom line:** the design is sound, the five deltas are the right shape, the failure modes they target are real and well-evidenced. I do not block canonicalization. I do ask the operator to consider resolving C1 and E1 before treating the design as final, and to address the granularity findings (A1–A2, B1–B4, D1–D4) as the design is implemented.","created_at":"2026-08-08T00:02:08.290785530Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:AeFJHIyTRksanmzpycvM9aj2nyQdBUS3PjMr42DfwDw","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg+8GjH29kCkE0AeTFAKvtfnq+J8Wx4jZpWHpxoWgRhc4AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA92ZNDqcWueGuVhAFcgrmXHkc0W5WFXZautcZCDx0q2Ox3SzApaBe6bROR01fCVtn9+knUzEhNq4j+DIGrjscDA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:AeFJHIyTRksanmzpycvM9aj2nyQdBUS3PjMr42DfwDw","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg+8GjH29kCkE0AeTFAKvtfnq+J8Wx4jZpWHpxoWgRhc4AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQEYVOvFyRgp6m0obYQrJJ4hYMcHSnFrqk0FEF5hTg0fMFBBNu+JQMVquKDjqDnicmQa7ZqQOBEAybPGrQZ+h/wM="}
